    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d785">

                <pb facs="0796" n="538" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg538"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/2101</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The German Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bernstorff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cedarhurst, N. Y.</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-09-08">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 8, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received September 9.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I have been waiting to
                    hear from home about the <hi rendition="#italic">Hesperian</hi> incident, but
                    have as yet not received any information, <pb facs="0797" n="539" xml:id="pg539"
                    /> which seems natural, as the submarines engaged at the time of the sinking of
                    the <hi rendition="#italic">Hesperian</hi> must first return home. Till then it
                    will not be possible to know whether the <hi rendition="#italic">Hesperian</hi>
                    was sunk by a German submarine or a German or British mine. However this may be,
                    I wish to inform you, that this case in no way changes our general policy with
                    regard to submarine warfare.</p>
                <p>I further desire to draw your attention to the fact that according to all reports
                    the <hi rendition="#italic">Hesperian</hi> was armed. This seems exceedingly
                    important as the whole controversy between the United States and Germany turns
                    on the subject of “unarmed merchant vessels.” Therefore, in my opinion, the case
                    of the <hi rendition="#italic">Hesperian</hi> has no bearing at all on the
                    question we have been discussing.</p>
                <p>I remain [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">J. Bernstorff</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
